  - { item_id: "pdd-pocket-audio-digital-disc-recorder-player", qty: 1, note: "The book says a disc player and headphones." }
trackable_resources: []
restrictions:
  - "Alignment: any."
  - "Attribute requirements: M.E. 10 and P.S., P.P. and P.E. 12 or higher, plus a competitive spirit."
  - "Hand to Hand: Basic cannot be improved except through an area of specialization."
  - "Cybernetics: none. They defeat the purpose of sport; a Sportsman who needs prosthetics generally considers his career over. Evil or selfish Sportsmen may cheat with small or secret devices and illegal performance enhancing chemicals, and are finished if caught."
extraction_notes: "WB19 Australia printed 115-118 (cache p116-p119, cache page = printed folio + 1), read off the text layer in column order. The entry heading Sportsman O.C.C. (An O.C.C. for any Australian location), its lore and the start of the Special Sportsman O.C.C. Abilities & Bonuses are on printed 115; the backgrounds end and the areas of expertise run across printed 116 and 117; Multiple Specializations, the adventure ideas and the stat block start on printed 117; the stat block ends with Standard Equipment, Money and Cybernetics on printed 118. || HEADING: no NPC or villain heading, so no evil tag. Listed under Optional City O.C.C.s on printed 105, so occ_group is optional. Base S.D.C. is printed as 3D6 inside ability 1 and stored as sdc_base, so no men_of_arms line is needed. No authored tag fits every specialty, so tags is the empty list. || XP: the Jackaroo, Roadganger & Sportsman ladder on the Experience Tables, printed 224, read off a render. || BACKGROUND (ability 1): the character takes the bonuses of the area he originated from; stored as a pick-one special_abilities group of three, each carrying its bonuses. Coastline/Rim prints P.E. twice (+1D4 to P.E., then +2 to P.E.); both are stored, as P.E. 1D4+2. Desert/Back of Beyond: the one-time +1D6 to starting Hit Points is a pool bonus. || STRONG MIND (ability 2): +1 to save vs mind control and illusions is mind_control 1 and a saves.other entry labelled vs illusions; +2 to save vs disease and pain stored as disease 2 and pain 2. || AREA OF EXPERTISE: the player selects one of nineteen at creation; each grants skills, so each is a variant with skills_additional. A variant''s bonuses replace the class''s, so every variant that states bonuses restates the Strong Mind saves. Stored per specialty, with what was left to prose: Archer (W.P. Archery and W.P. Targeting; the +1 strike and extra bow attack are conditional on the bow). Ball Sports (+1D4 S.D.C.; Running with its extra +1D6 Spd stored as an attribute bonus; a choice of W.P. Small Thrown Weapons or W.P. Blunt; the +2 initiative and +1 strike are read as applying when using a bat, club or other striking weapon and are prose). Bicyclist (+1D4 P.E., +1D4 Spd, +1 initiative; General Repair & Maintenance for the printed Basic Maintenance/Repairs, Running, Bicycling at 64 for Cycling +20%; +1 dodge on a bike is prose). Professional Boxer (Boxing; +1 P.E., +1D6 S.D.C., +1 initiative, +1 strike, +2 disarm, +4 pull punch). Gladiator (+1 initiative, +2 disarm, +1 parry, +3 vs Horror Factor, +2D6 S.D.C.; Hand to Hand: Expert, Boxing, W.P. Paired Weapons, three ancient W.P.s and one modern, stored as two choice groups over the whole W.P. category because the catalog does not split ancient from modern). Martial Artist (Hand to Hand: Martial Arts; +1 P.P., +2D6 S.D.C., +1D4 initiative, +2 pull punch, +2 disarm). Professional Wrestler (Hand to Hand: Expert, Wrestling; +1 P.E., +1 initiative, +1 disarm, +1 roll, +3 pull punch). Football (+2D6 S.D.C., +1D4 initiative, +1 strike, +2 roll; Running and a choice of Gymnastics or Wrestling). Motor Sports (+2 initiative, +1D4 M.E.; Automobile and Motorcycles & Snowmobiles at +20%, Combat Driving for the printed Australian Combat Driving, Automotive Mechanics +5%). Mountain Climber (Climbing +15%; +1D6 P.S. and +1D6 P.E.). Pro-Gymnastics (Gymnastics; +1D4 P.S., +1 P.E., +1 P.B., +1 initiative, +1 dodge, +1 roll). Pro-Acrobat (Acrobatics; +1 M.A., +2 P.S., +1D4 P.P., +1 initiative, +1 strike). Sailing (Boat: Sail Type +15%, Navigation, Swimming). Surfer & Swimmer (the book prints +2 to P.P. and then +1 to P.P. and P.E.; both are stored, as P.P. 3 and P.E. 1; +1 roll, +1D6 S.D.C.; Swimming +15%, Water Skiing & Surfing for Surfing, Gymnastics +5%; the +1D4 to M.A. or P.B. is a choice and is prose). Swimmer & Diver (+2 P.S., +2 P.E., +1D4 P.B., +3 roll, +1D6 S.D.C.; Swimming +15%, SCUBA +10%, Acrobatics +5%). Big Game/Trophy Hunter (W.P. Bolt Action Rifle, W.P. Energy Rifle, Track & Trap Animals +10% for the printed Track Animals, Wilderness Survival +5%). Target Shooter (W.P. Revolver, W.P. Automatic Pistol for the printed W.P. Pistol, W.P. Bolt Action Rifle, Sniper; +2 initiative; +1 on aimed shots is prose). Track and Field (+2 P.E., +2D6 Spd, +2 initiative, +1D6 S.D.C.; Running with an extra +2D6 Spd, so Spd is stored as 4D6; Gymnastics). White Water Sports (+1 P.E., +1D6 S.D.C., +1 initiative; Swimming +5%, Boat: Paddle Types/Canoe/Kayak at +15% for Pilot: Kayaking, and Boat: Motor, Race & Hydrofoil for the printed Pilot: (small) Boats). || NOT STORED: Pilot: Race Car (Motor Sports) has no catalog row and is noted on the Automobile entry. Multiple Specializations (a second or third area of expertise at halved bonuses and fewer related skills) is prose in special_abilities and GM Notes; the stored character holds one specialty. The Martial Artist''s alternative of a World Book 8: Japan or Ninjas & Superspies martial art is in the skill note. || SKILLS: Speaks English at 80+3D6% is Language: Australian English at 80, and Basic Math (+3D6%) is Mathematics: Basic at the catalog base; in both the 3D6 is rolled and added by hand, because a skill base cannot hold a die. Dance or Cook (+10%) is a choice of the two. Hand to Hand: Basic with no room for improvement unless a specialization says otherwise is stored as an empty price list. || RELATED: eight at level one and one more at levels 2, 4, 8 and 12. Pilot except Robot Elite, Jet Aircraft, Jet Fighter or military vehicles is stored as the Robot Combat Elite rows, Jet Aircraft and the five Military rows of the Pilot category. Science Math only is read as both Mathematics rows. Cowboy, Espionage, Medical and Military are printed None and left out. The stat block prints the Alignment line twice. || MONEY: 2D4x300 dollars stored (printed 2D4x$300; Australian dollars equal credits). The 3,000 dollars to spend on adventuring equipment and the NPC figure (three times as much, half in tradeable items) are in GM Notes. || EQUIPMENT not stored as rows: the sporting equipment of the chosen sport (weapons, protective gear, uniforms, a bicycle or surfboard), the weight set, a book or two on the sport and the 1D4 trophies have no row. The disc player and headphones use the nearest catalog row. || NOT STORED: the adventure ideas and the list of common sports in Rifts Australia are background."
---

## Lore

Australia has always been a competitive, sport-loving country, and the
Sportsman is someone with the will and drive to build a professional
athlete''s body: a runner, footballer, boxer, wrestler, martial artist,
cyclist, shooter, sailor or surfer. The stereotype is the high-tech, snobby
city jock who thinks a great deal of himself, though most are well
educated.

In Melbourne and Perth nearly every event is televised and athletes are
respected and often idolized. They have gyms, coaches, good food, health
care and safe, tightly ruled sports. The Outback has little time for play,
so its athletes are amateurs who train for the love of it, or the fighters
and racers of rough local contests, arena bouts and Roadganger blood
sports.

The player chooses where the character comes from (the coast or Rim, the
desert, or a Tech-City) and one area of sport to be a professional in. The
book notes that the class can be transplanted to any modern setting.

## GM Notes

Background and specialty are both chosen at creation. The sheet applies the
background''s attribute, S.D.C. and Hit Point bonuses, and the specialty''s
skills and unconditional bonuses. Add these by hand: 3D6 to Speak English
and 3D6 to Basic Math; the Surfer & Swimmer''s +1D4 to M.A. or P.B.; and the
conditional items listed under Specialty Bonuses Not on the Sheet.

Two figures are stored as the book prints them and look like misprints.
Coastline/Rim gives P.E. twice (+1D4 and +2). Surfer & Swimmer gives P.P.
twice (+2 and +1). Both grants are applied; the G.M. may rule otherwise.

Multiple specializations: a second area of expertise halves the O.C.C.
Related Skill picks and halves the second specialty''s bonuses, rounding
down (a +1 is lost); a third leaves two O.C.C. Related Skills and halves
the third specialty''s bonuses. Skills and skill bonuses from the extra
specialties are unchanged. The sheet stores one specialty only.

Boxers and Gladiators may buy automatic dodge for two Secondary Skills. A
Martial Artist may instead spend two O.C.C. Related Skills or four
Secondary Skills on a martial art from Rifts World Book 8: Japan or a
modified one from Ninjas & Superspies, without Oriental Mystic Powers.
Motor Sports also prints Pilot: Race Car (+20%), which the catalog has no
skill for.

Equipment the sheet does not list: everything the chosen sport needs
(weapons, protective gear, a few uniforms, and a bicycle, surfboard or
other vehicle where the sport calls for one), a weight set, a book or two
on the sport, and perhaps 1D4 trophies. Adventuring equipment is up to the
G.M.; the player may spend $3000 on it.

Money: 2D4x300 dollars. NPCs have three times as much, half of it in
tradeable items. Australian dollars equal credits.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'sportsman');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'sportsman';

-- Records this run. REQUIRED: the smoke test fails a data script that has no
-- footer, or whose footer names a different file.
INSERT INTO data_script_runs (filename) VALUES ('add-sportsman-class.sql');
